Differentiate the equation repeatedly:
- $y'=y^2-1+\cos x$, so $y'(0)=0-1+1=0$;
- $y''=2yy'-\sin x$, so $y''(0)=0$;
- $y'''=2(y')^2+2yy''-\cos x$, so $y'''(0)=-1$.

Thus $y'(0)=y''(0)=0$ and $y'''(0)<0$. The function $y'$ has $y'(0)=0$, $(y')'(0)=0$, $(y')''(0)=-1<0$, so $y'$ has a strict local maximum $0$ at $x=0$: $y'<0$ on $(-\delta,0)\cup(0,\delta)$. Hence $y$ is strictly decreasing on $(-\delta,\delta)$ (and has no extremum at $0$). Indeed $y\approx-\frac{x^3}6$.

